385qelectronic book  z97830316853787 a10.1007/978-3-031-68538-52doi  aspon1481626947  a(OCoLC)1481626947  aGW5XEbengerdaepncGW5XEdYDXdCStdUtOrBLW  aMAIN 4aQA76.9.M35bY85 2025 7aUYA2bicssc 7aCOM0140002bisacsh 7aUYA2thema04a004.01/51223/eng/202412201 aYukita, Shuichi,eauthor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no200211291110aCategory theory using Haskell :ban introduction with Moggi and Yoneda /cShuichi Yukita 1aCham :bBirkhäuser,c[2025]  a1 online resource (xi, 297 pages) :billustrations (some color)  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aComputer science foundations and applied logic,x2731-5762  aIncludes bibliographical references and index  aThis unique book offers an introductory course on category theory, which became a working language in algebraic geometry and number theory in the 1950s and began to spread to logic and computer science soon after it was created. Offering excellent use of helpful examples in Haskell, the work covers (among other things) concepts of functors, natural transformations, monads, adjoints, universality, category equivalence, and many others. The main goal is to understand the Yoneda lemma, which can be used to reverse-engineer the implementation of a function. Later chapters offer more insights into computer science, including computation with output, nondeterministic computation, and continuation passing. Topics and features: Contains rigorous mathematical arguments to support the theory Provides numerous Haskell code-implementing examples Engages with plentiful diagram chasing, with special emphasis on the design patterns for constructing a large diagram out of basic small pieces Offers insights into category theory to quantum computing and the foundation of computing discipline Serves as a preparatory course for monoidal categories and higher categories The work will be useful to undergraduate students in computer science who have enough background in college mathematics such as linear algebra and basics in Haskell polymorphic functions. Further, it will appeal to graduate students and researchers in computing disciplines who want to newly acquire serious knowledge of category theory. Prof. Shuichi Yukita is with the Faculty of Computer and Information Sciences at Hosei University, Hosei, Japan0 a1. Catetory, Functor, Natural Transformation -- 2. Equivalence of categories -- 3. Universality and Limits -- 4. Functors and Limits -- 5. Adjoints -- 6. Monads -- 7. Representable Functors  aDescription based on online resource; title from digital title page (viewed on December 20, 2024) 0aCategories (Mathematics)0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85020992 0aHaskell (Computer program language)0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh93003496 0aComputer sciencexMathematics.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85042295 0aComputer science foundations and applied logic.40zAvailable to Stanford-affiliated users.uhttps://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-031-68538-5xWMSySpringerLinkxProvider: SpringerxpurchasedxeLoaderURLxsp4xspon1481626947  aeLoaderRecord xsp4  a92bSTFffi00486d4e-6fbe-4568-ac1d-52166361d87cse705c7fc-d65c-46e2-bed0-b8c5809cb75d02371cam a2200481 i in0000024196220250403134755.0150311r20041uuucc            000 0 chi d  a(OCoLC)ocn904660685  a(OCoLC)904660685  aHUAbengerdacHUAdOCLCFdOCLCOdOCLdOCLCQdOCLCOdOCLCLdOCLCQdUtOrBLW  aa-cc---  aDS753b.M476 2004 v. 831 6880-01aYin, Shidan,d1522-1582,eauthor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no20080156651https://id.oclc.org/worldcat/entity/E39PBJrgFMB6FTPpWgTrw4kYyd106880-02aYin Wentong gong Jin yu shan fang gao :b[yi juan] /cYin Shidan zhuan176880-03aYin Wentong gong ji  6880-04aDi 1 ban 16880-05aBeijing Shi :bXian zhuang shu ju,c2004  apages 325-342 ;c26 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 6