asters -- Recognizing alternative management approaches -- Implementing initial response measures -- Caring for the injured, dead, and distraught -- Managing public information, donations, and volunteers -- Moving beyond immediate needs -- Promoting recovery and mitigation -- Overcoming typical challenges -- Harnessing technology and organization -- Foreseeing the future -- Enhancing disaster resilience  aProvides an overview of the various types of disasters that mayoccur, the myriad of actors that are involved in emergencymanagement, and the diverse theoretical frameworks from whichpost-disaster activities may be approached.- Includes updated chapters keeping the material freshand accessible while incorporating recent changes in policy and newresearch- Highlights lessons learned from such incidents andcatastrophes as: Hurricane Katrina, Earthquake in Haiti, Parkinglot collapse in Miami, West Texas industrial explosion, Train wreckin Spain, Aurora movie shooting, Times Square bombing, etc.- Examines typical challenges to be expected duringresponse efforts along with tools and techniques to enhance theability to protect lives, reduce property damage and minimizedisruption- Includes an instructor package which contains: Sample Syllabus, Teaching Suggestions, Test bank, and PowerPoints 0aDisaster reliefzUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85038302 0aDisasters.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8503830310aDisasters.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8503830322aRelief Workxorganization & administration.20aCivil defensexorganization & administration.20aEmergency medical servicesxorganization & administration. 0aUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n78095330 6aCatastrophes. 7adisasters.2aat 7aSOCIAL SCIENCExSociologyxGeneral.2bisacsh 7aDisasters.2fast0http://id.worldcat.org/fast/894782 7aDisaster relief.2fast0http://id.worldcat.org/fast/894731 7aUnited States.2fast0http://id.worl